/** Generated from the overhaul textbook. Re-run tools/import-overhaul-courses.mjs. Do not edit by hand. */
export default {"id":"sounddesign-level-4","number":4,"title":"Envelopes","skillBand":"Textbook","duration":"From the course textbook","summary":"- Define an envelope as a deterministic, unipolar, one-shot control voltage/function generator triggered by MIDI note-on and note-off events. - Formulate and apply the fundamental rule of envelope architecture: Attack, Decay, and Release ar","modules":[{"id":"sounddesign-m-4-1-1","code":"4.1","title":"ADSR Truth","blurb":"- Define an envelope as a deterministic, unipolar, one-shot control voltage/function generator triggered by MIDI note-on and note-off events. - Formulate and apply the fundamental rule of envelope architecture: Attack, Decay, and Release ar","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"sounddesign-l-o-10","title":"Section overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Learning Objectives","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Define an <strong>envelope</strong> as a deterministic, unipolar, one-shot control voltage/function generator triggered by MIDI note-on and note-off events.","Formulate and apply the fundamental rule of envelope architecture: <strong>Attack, Decay, and Release are TIME durations; Sustain is an AMPLITUDE LEVEL</strong>.","Control the acoustic transition between instant transient punch (0 ms - 5 ms) and gradual pad swells (&gt;300 ms).","Analyze and manipulate envelope curvature (Linear, Exponential, Logarithmic) to maximize acoustic punch and natural dynamic decay.","Navigate extended envelope topologies including <strong>Hold</strong>, <strong>Delay</strong>, and <strong>Looping Envelopes</strong> (<strong>DAHDSR</strong>)."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-4-1-1","title":"4.1.1 — What an Envelope Is","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.1.1 — What an Envelope Is","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"In synthesis, an <strong>envelope</strong> is an automated function generator that outputs a dynamic control signal over time whenever a MIDI note is triggered. "},{"type":"prose","html":"Unlike a free-running LFO (which cycles continuously in a repeating loop), an envelope is a <strong>one-shot trigger</strong>: it initiates its sequence precisely when a key is pressed (Note-On) and completes its cycle when the key is released (Note-Off)."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">MIDI Event Timeline:\nNote-On (Key Pressed)                                  Note-Off (Key Released)\n   |                                                              |\n   v                                                              v\n+-------------------------------------------------------------+   +---------------+\n| ATTACK STAGE             | DECAY STAGE      | SUSTAIN STAGE |   | RELEASE STAGE |\n| (Level rises to peak)    | (Falls to level) | (Held steady) |   | (Falls to zero)|\n+--------------------------+------------------+---------------+---+---------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"Envelopes do not produce sound on their own. They generate control values (0.0 to 1.0 or 0% to 100%) that are routed to modulate volume (Amplifier), brightness (Filter Cutoff), pitch (Oscillators), or modulation depth."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ultimate Sound Design: Mastering Sound Design</em>, Section 4.1 (Envelope Fundamentals)","<em>PML Synthesis – 4 – Modulators</em>, Chapter 1"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-4-1-2","title":"4.1.2 — The ADSR Truth: Sustain is a LEVEL, Not a Time","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.1.2 — The ADSR Truth: Sustain is a LEVEL, Not a Time","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The single most prevalent error among beginner producers is treating Sustain as a duration (\"how long the sound sustains\"). In synthesizer architecture, <strong>Sustain is strictly an AMPLITUDE LEVEL; Attack, Decay, and Release are TIME DURATIONS.</strong>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">Amplitude (%)\n 100% |        /\\ [PEAK LEVEL: 100%]\n      |       /  \\\n      |      /    \\\n  S%  |     /      \\==================== [SUSTAIN: An Amplitude Level (% or dB)]\n      |    /                             (Maintained indefinitely as long as key is held)\n      |   /                                                 \\\n  0%  |--+------+---+----------------------------------------\\----+----&gt; Time (s)\n      |&lt;-- A --&gt;|&lt; D&gt;|                                       |&lt;-R-&gt;|\n        [TIME]   [TIME]                                      [TIME]</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+----------+-----------------------+-------------------+----------------------------------------+\n| Stage    | Parameter Domain      | Standard Range    | What It Controls                       |\n+----------+-----------------------+-------------------+----------------------------------------+\n| **A**    | **TIME** (Duration)   | $0\\text{ ms} - 20\\text{ s}$| Time taken to rise from $0$ to peak.  |\n+----------+-----------------------+-------------------+----------------------------------------+\n| **D**    | **TIME** (Duration)   | $1\\text{ ms} - 20\\text{ s}$| Time taken to fall from peak to Sustain|\n+----------+-----------------------+-------------------+----------------------------------------+\n| **S**    | **LEVEL** (Amplitude) | $0\\% - 100\\%$     | The static amplitude level held while  |\n|          |                       | ($-\\text{inf} \\text{ to } 0\\text{ dB}$)| the MIDI note remains held down. |\n+----------+-----------------------+-------------------+----------------------------------------+\n| **R**    | **TIME** (Duration)   | $1\\text{ ms} - 30\\text{ s}$| Time taken to fall from Sustain to zero|\n|          |                       |                   | after Note-Off (key release).          |\n+----------+-----------------------+-------------------+----------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>The Sustain Rule:</strong> If you release a MIDI key, the envelope instantly bypasses the remainder of the Decay and Sustain stages and jumps immediately to the <strong>Release</strong> stage."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ultimate Sound Design: Mastering Sound Design</em>, Section 4.2 (ADSR Nomenclature)","<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §3"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-4-1-3","title":"4.1.3 — Attack Time: 0 ms Punch vs. Pad Swells","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.1.3 — Attack Time: 0 ms Punch vs. Pad Swells","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The <strong>Attack Time</strong> dictates the velocity of the initial note onset:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">Instant Attack (0 - 2 ms):                    Slow Attack (300 ms - 3 s):\nAmplitude                                     Amplitude\n  1.0 | |                                       1.0 |            /\n      | |                                           |           /\n  0.0 +-+----------------&gt; Time                 0.0 +----------/---------&gt; Time\n      0 ms                                                 300 ms\n  [Immediate Transient Crack]                      [Smooth Acoustic Swell / Fade-In]</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Instant Attack (0 ms - 2 ms):</strong> The sound reaches full volume instantaneously. Required for punchy kicks, snappy bass plucks, and aggressive stabs.","<em>Technical Warning:</em> An attack time of literally 0 ms on a non-zero-crossing waveform can produce an unwanted digital click or pop. Setting attack to 1-3 ms eliminates clicks while retaining maximum transient punch.","<strong>Slow Attack (200 ms - 3 s):</strong> The sound fades in gradually. Essential for ambient pads, bowed strings, slow brass swells, and cinematic textures."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>PML Synthesis – 4 – Modulators</em>, Chapter 2 (Attack Topologies)","<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §3"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-4-1-4","title":"4.1.4 — Decay Time: The Slope Down to Sustain","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.1.4 — Decay Time: The Slope Down to Sustain","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The <strong>Decay Time</strong> controls how long the sound takes to drop from its initial maximum peak (100%) down to the steady-state Sustain level (S)."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">High Sustain (Organ / Drone):                 Zero Sustain (Pluck / Percussion):\nAmplitude                                     Amplitude\n  1.0 |  /\\                                     1.0 |  /\\\n      | /  \\==================== [S = 80%]          | /  \\\n  0.0 +----+-------------------&gt; Time           0.0 +----+-\\-------------&gt; Time\n          Decay                                         Decay  [S = 0%] (Note dies out)</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>If Sustain is at 100% (0 dB):</strong> The Decay knob has <strong>zero audible effect</strong>, because the peak level and sustain level are identical.","<strong>If Sustain is at 0% (-inf dB):</strong> The Decay knob becomes the <strong>master duration control</strong> of the sound. Once Decay completes, the sound goes completely silent, even if you continue holding the key down for 10 minutes (the definition of a <em>pluck</em> or <em>drum hit</em>)."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ultimate Sound Design: Mastering Sound Design</em>, Section 4.3 (Decay Mechanics)","<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §3"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-4-1-5","title":"4.1.5 — Release Time: What Happens After Key-Off","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.1.5 — Release Time: What Happens After Key-Off","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The <strong>Release Time</strong> governs the acoustic \"tail\" of the sound once a MIDI key is released (Note-Off)."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">Note-On               Note-Off (Key Released)\n   |                     |\n   v                     v\nAmplitude\n  1.0 |  /\\\n      | /  \\=============\n  0.0 +----+-------------+----\\---------&gt; Time\n                              |&lt;-R-&gt;|\n                              [Release Tail]</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Short Release (10 ms - 50 ms):</strong> The note stops immediately when you let go of the key. Provides tight, clinical, staccato articulation for basslines and fast rhythmic riffs.","<strong>Long Release (1 s - 8 s):</strong> The note rings out with a long pseudo-reverberant tail. Essential for bells, atmospheric pads, piano emulations, and ambient soundscapes."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Caution:</strong> Long release times on low-frequency bass patches cause overlapping notes that produce severe low-end phase cancellation and mud during fast melodic passages."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ultimate Sound Design: Mastering Sound Design</em>, Section 4.4 (Release Mechanics)","<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §3"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-4-1-6","title":"4.1.6 — Envelope Curves: Linear vs. Exponential vs. Logarithmic","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.1.6 — Envelope Curves: Linear vs. Exponential vs. Logarithmic","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"In modern synthesizers (<code>Vital</code>, <code>Surge XT</code>, <code>Drift</code>), the slope between envelope stages is not restricted to a straight line; it can be curved continuously via Bezier control points:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">Linear (Straight):              Exponential (Concave):          Logarithmic (Convex):\nAmplitude                       Amplitude                       Amplitude\n  1.0 |   \\                       1.0 |   \\                       1.0 |   -----\\\n      |    \\                          |    \\                          |         \\\n  0.0 +-----\\-----&gt; Time          0.0 +------\\----&gt; Time          0.0 +----------\\-&gt; Time\n     [Robotic / Mechanical]          [Snappy / Physical Punch]       [Heavy / Sustained Body]</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Exponential Decay (Concave Curve):</strong> The level drops rapidly at first, then tapers off slowly as it approaches zero. This matches the physical acoustic behavior of real-world vibrating objects (drums, plucked guitar strings, bells). <strong>This is the secret to making synthetic plucks and kicks sound snappy and punchy.</strong>","<strong>Logarithmic Decay (Convex Curve):</strong> The level stays high for most of the duration before dropping abruptly. Useful for gating effects and synthetic brass swells."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Vital User Manual</em>, Section 5 (Bezier Envelope Curves)","<em>Surge XT User Manual</em>, Section 5 (Envelope Shapes)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-4-1-7","title":"4.1.7 — Extended Topologies: AHDSR and DAHDSR","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.1.7 — Extended Topologies: AHDSR and DAHDSR","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Advanced sound design engines feature extended envelope topologies that insert dedicated stages around the core ADSR framework:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">[ DELAY ] ---&gt; [ ATTACK ] ---&gt; [ HOLD ] ---&gt; [ DECAY ] ---&gt; [ SUSTAIN ] ---&gt; [ RELEASE ]\n   |                              |\n   v                              v\nPauses before onset           Forces peak level to hold\n                              for a fixed duration</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+----------+-----------------------+------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Stage    | Full Name             | Sound Design Function                                      |\n+----------+-----------------------+------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **D**    | **Delay**             | Time delay before the Attack phase begins. Used to offset  |\n|          |                       | modulation layers in multi-oscillator patches.             |\n+----------+-----------------------+------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **H**    | **Hold**              | Holds the Attack peak at $100\\%$ volume for a fixed duration|\n|          |                       | before Decay initiates. Essential for punchy synth drums.  |\n+----------+-----------------------+------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Loop** | **Looping Envelope**  | Automatically re-triggers the A-D-S cycle, transforming   |\n|          |                       | the envelope into a complex custom LFO (`Analog`, `Vital`).|\n+----------+-----------------------+------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Surge XT User Manual</em>, Section 5 (Flexible Envelope Generators)","<em>Ableton Live 12 Reference Manual</em>, Chapter 23 (Analog Envelope Modes)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"sounddesign-m-4-2-2","code":"4.2","title":"Amp vs. Filter Envelope","blurb":"- Differentiate the acoustic role of the Amplifier Envelope (AEG / VCA) from the Filter Envelope (FEG / VCF). - Formulate the fundamental rule of filter envelope operation: FEG requires a low static filter cutoff frequency and positive enve","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"sounddesign-l-o-11","title":"Section overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Learning Objectives","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Differentiate the acoustic role of the <strong>Amplifier Envelope (AEG / VCA)</strong> from the <strong>Filter Envelope (FEG / VCF)</strong>.","Formulate the fundamental rule of filter envelope operation: <strong>FEG requires a low static filter cutoff frequency and positive envelope depth to create audible movement</strong>.","Apply <strong>Velocity to Envelope Modulation</strong> to make synth patches respond dynamically to keyboard playing intensity.","Construct the four foundational synthesizer role shapes from scratch: <strong>Pluck</strong>, <strong>Pad</strong>, <strong>Organ</strong>, and <strong>Synth Bass</strong>."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-4-2-1","title":"4.2.1 — The Amplifier Envelope (AEG): Volume Contour","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.2.1 — The Amplifier Envelope (AEG): Volume Contour","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The <strong>Amplifier Envelope Generator (AEG)</strong> controls the master Voltage-Controlled Amplifier (VCA). Its exclusive role is to dictate <strong>loudness over time</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">[ OSCILLATOR ] ===&gt; [ FILTER ] ===&gt; [ VCA AMPLIFIER ] ===&gt; [ AUDIO OUT ]\n                                            ^\n                                            |\n                                  [ AMP ENVELOPE (AEG) ]\n                                  (Controls Volume Contour)</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["When AEG is at 0%, the instrument is completely silent.","When AEG rises to 100%, the instrument outputs maximum volume.","Without an AEG, an oscillator would either drone indefinitely or click abruptly on/off like an electric light switch."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ultimate Sound Design: Mastering Sound Design</em>, Section 4.5 (Amplifier Envelopes)","<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §3"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-4-2-2","title":"4.2.2 — The Filter Envelope (FEG): Brightness Contour","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.2.2 — The Filter Envelope (FEG): Brightness Contour","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The <strong>Filter Envelope Generator (FEG)</strong> is routed directly to the Filter Cutoff Frequency (f_c). Its exclusive role is to dictate <strong>timbral brightness over time</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">[ OSCILLATOR ] ===&gt; [ FILTER (VCF) ] ===&gt; [ AMPLIFIER (VCA) ] ===&gt; [ AUDIO OUT ]\n                           ^\n                           |\n                 [ FILTER ENVELOPE (FEG) ]\n                 (Moves Cutoff Up and Down)</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["When the FEG triggers, it pushes the filter cutoff frequency <em>upward</em> from its resting position during the Attack phase, and allows it to slide <em>downward</em> during the Decay phase.","The human ear perceives this as a note striking with sharp brightness and immediately softening into a darker, warmer body."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ultimate Sound Design: Mastering Sound Design</em>, Section 4.6 (Filter Envelopes)","<em>PML Synthesis – 4 – Modulators</em>, Chapter 3"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-4-2-3","title":"4.2.3 — Envelope Amount (Env Depth / Velocity to Env)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.2.3 — Envelope Amount (Env Depth / Velocity to Env)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The <strong>Filter Envelope Amount</strong> (also labeled <em>Env Depth</em>, <em>Filter Amt</em>, or <em>Mod Depth</em>) controls <strong>how far in Hertz</strong> the envelope pushes the filter cutoff above its base resting point."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">Cutoff Frequency (Hz)\n20 kHz |          /\\ [Peak Reached with High Env Amount (+5.0)]\n       |         /  \\\n 2 kHz |        /    \\ [Peak Reached with Low Env Amount (+1.0)]\n       |       /      \\\n300 Hz | =====+========+==================== [Base Static Cutoff (Resting Position)]\n       +------------------------------------&gt; Time</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Positive Envelope Amount (+100%):</strong> The cutoff opens upward on note-on and falls back down during decay.","<strong>Negative Envelope Amount (-100% / Inverted):</strong> The cutoff drops downward on note-on (darkening the onset) and rises back up during decay.","<strong>Velocity to Envelope:</strong> Maps MIDI key strike strength (Velocity 1-127) to the Envelope Amount. Soft key strikes produce a dark, muted pluck; hard key strikes force the filter cutoff fully open for an explosive, bright transient."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>PML Synthesis – 4 – Modulators</em>, Chapter 4 (Envelope Depth &amp; Dynamics)","<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §3 &amp; §4"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-4-2-4","title":"4.2.4 — The Golden Rule: Base Cutoff Must Be Low for FEG to Work","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.2.4 — The Golden Rule: Base Cutoff Must Be Low for FEG to Work","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The most common mistake when programming filter envelopes is leaving the static Filter Cutoff knob fully open (20 kHz) and wondering why the Filter Envelope Decay knob has zero audible effect."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">MISTAKE (Cutoff Already at 20 kHz):\n20 kHz | ==================================== [Base Cutoff at Max -&gt; Filter is already 100% open]\n       | (FEG tries to open the filter further, but there are no higher frequencies to reveal!)\n       +------------------------------------&gt;\n\nCORRECT (Base Cutoff Set Low at 200 Hz):\n20 kHz |          /\\\n       |         /  \\  [FEG successfully sweeps across the entire harmonic spectrum]\n200 Hz | ========+===+======================= [Base Cutoff Low -&gt; Huge range of dynamic travel]\n       +------------------------------------&gt;</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>The Golden Rule:</strong> To hear a filter envelope open and close, <strong>the static Filter Cutoff knob must be set LOW (100 Hz - 800 Hz)</strong>. The base cutoff defines the starting floor; the Envelope Amount dictates how high the ceiling reaches."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ultimate Sound Design: Mastering Sound Design</em>, Section 4.7 (The Filter Floor Law)","<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §3"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-4-2-5","title":"4.2.5 — The Four Foundation Shapes: Pluck Recipe","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.2.5 — The Four Foundation Shapes: Pluck Recipe","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"A <strong>Pluck</strong> is defined by an instant transient attack followed by a fast dynamic decay down to silence with zero sustain level."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">PLUCK ENVELOPE PROFILE:\nAmplitude / Cutoff\n  1.0 | |\\\n      | | \\\n  0.0 +-+--\\-----------------------------&gt; Time\n       A  D  [Sustain = 0.0]</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+------------------+-----------------------+------------------------------------+\n| Parameter        | Amp Envelope (AEG)    | Filter Envelope (FEG)              |\n+------------------+-----------------------+------------------------------------+\n| **Attack**       | $0\\text{ ms} - 2\\text{ ms}$ (Instant)| $0\\text{ ms} - 5\\text{ ms}$ (Snappy crack)|\n+------------------+-----------------------+------------------------------------+\n| **Decay**        | $150\\text{ ms} - 400\\text{ ms}$| $100\\text{ ms} - 300\\text{ ms}$   |\n+------------------+-----------------------+------------------------------------+\n| **Sustain**      | **$0.0$ ($-\\text{inf dB}$)** (Zero)| **$0.0$ (Zero)**             |\n+------------------+-----------------------+------------------------------------+\n| **Release**      | $50\\text{ ms} - 150\\text{ ms}$| $50\\text{ ms} - 150\\text{ ms}$    |\n+------------------+-----------------------+------------------------------------+\n| **Filter Base**  | —                     | Low ($200\\text{ Hz} - 400\\text{ Hz}$)|\n+------------------+-----------------------+------------------------------------+\n| **Env Amount**   | —                     | High ($+60\\% \\text{ to } +100\\%$)  |\n+------------------+-----------------------+------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §12 (Recipe B)","<em>PML Synthesis – 4 – Modulators</em>, Chapter 5"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-4-2-6","title":"4.2.6 — The Four Foundation Shapes: Pad Recipe","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.2.6 — The Four Foundation Shapes: Pad Recipe","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"A <strong>Pad</strong> is an ambient harmonic cushion designed to swell gently into the arrangement and sustain continuously across chord changes."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">PAD ENVELOPE PROFILE:\nAmplitude / Cutoff\n  1.0 |            /====================\\\n      |           /                      \\\n  0.0 +----------/------------------------\\-&gt; Time\n              Attack                      Release</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+------------------+-----------------------+------------------------------------+\n| Parameter        | Amp Envelope (AEG)    | Filter Envelope (FEG)              |\n+------------------+-----------------------+------------------------------------+\n| **Attack**       | $300\\text{ ms} - 2.0\\text{ s}$ (Slow)| $500\\text{ ms} - 2.5\\text{ s}$ (Bloom)|\n+------------------+-----------------------+------------------------------------+\n| **Decay**        | $500\\text{ ms} - 1.5\\text{ s}$| $1.0\\text{ s} - 3.0\\text{ s}$      |\n+------------------+-----------------------+------------------------------------+\n| **Sustain**      | **$80\\% - 100\\%$ (High)**| **$60\\% - 90\\%$ (High)**        |\n+------------------+-----------------------+------------------------------------+\n| **Release**      | $1.0\\text{ s} - 4.0\\text{ s}$ (Long)| $1.0\\text{ s} - 3.0\\text{ s}$|\n+------------------+-----------------------+------------------------------------+\n| **Filter Base**  | —                     | Medium ($400\\text{ Hz} - 1.2\\text{ kHz}$)|\n+------------------+-----------------------+------------------------------------+\n| **Env Amount**   | —                     | Moderate ($+20\\% \\text{ to } +50\\%$)|\n+------------------+-----------------------+------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §12 (Recipe A)","<em>PML Synthesis – 4 – Modulators</em>, Chapter 6"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-4-2-7","title":"4.2.7 — The Four Foundation Shapes: Organ Recipe","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.2.7 — The Four Foundation Shapes: Organ Recipe","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"An <strong>Organ</strong> (or continuous key) has zero dynamic decay; it strikes immediately, maintains 100% volume as long as the key is held, and stops instantly upon key release."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">ORGAN ENVELOPE PROFILE:\nAmplitude / Cutoff\n  1.0 | |===============================|\n      | |                               |\n  0.0 +-+-------------------------------+-&gt; Time\n        A                               R (Instant cut)</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+------------------+-----------------------+------------------------------------+\n| Parameter        | Amp Envelope (AEG)    | Filter Envelope (FEG)              |\n+------------------+-----------------------+------------------------------------+\n| **Attack**       | $0\\text{ ms} - 2\\text{ ms}$ (Instant)| $0\\text{ ms}$ (Instant)    |\n+------------------+-----------------------+------------------------------------+\n| **Decay**        | Irrelevant (Sustain is 100%)| Irrelevant                   |\n+------------------+-----------------------+------------------------------------+\n| **Sustain**      | **$100\\%$ ($0\\text{ dB}$)** (Maximum)| **$100\\%$ (Maximum)**      |\n+------------------+-----------------------+------------------------------------+\n| **Release**      | $1\\text{ ms} - 10\\text{ ms}$ (Instant)| $1\\text{ ms} - 10\\text{ ms}$|\n+------------------+-----------------------+------------------------------------+\n| **Filter Base**  | —                     | Open ($8\\text{ kHz} - 20\\text{ kHz}$)|\n+------------------+-----------------------+------------------------------------+\n| **Env Amount**   | —                     | $0\\%$ (Disabled / Static)          |\n+------------------+-----------------------+------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §3 &amp; §8"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-4-2-8","title":"4.2.8 — The Four Foundation Shapes: Bass Recipe","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.2.8 — The Four Foundation Shapes: Bass Recipe","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"A modern electronic <strong>Synth Bass</strong> requires a razor-sharp transient attack, punchy decay down to a controlled sustain level for held bass notes, and a tight release to prevent low-end mud."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">BASS ENVELOPE PROFILE:\nAmplitude / Cutoff\n  1.0 | |\\\n      | | \\==================== [Sustain = 50%]\n  0.0 +-+--+-------------------\\---------&gt; Time\n        A  D                   R (Tight: 30 ms)</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+------------------+-----------------------+------------------------------------+\n| Parameter        | Amp Envelope (AEG)    | Filter Envelope (FEG)              |\n+------------------+-----------------------+------------------------------------+\n| **Attack**       | $0\\text{ ms} - 2\\text{ ms}$ (Instant)| $0\\text{ ms} - 2\\text{ ms}$ (Transient)|\n+------------------+-----------------------+------------------------------------+\n| **Decay**        | $200\\text{ ms} - 500\\text{ ms}$| $80\\text{ ms} - 250\\text{ ms}$ (Punch)|\n+------------------+-----------------------+------------------------------------+\n| **Sustain**      | **$40\\% - 70\\%$ (Moderate)**| **$0\\% - 30\\%$ (Low)**          |\n+------------------+-----------------------+------------------------------------+\n| **Release**      | $20\\text{ ms} - 50\\text{ ms}$ (Tight)| $20\\text{ ms} - 50\\text{ ms}$|\n+------------------+-----------------------+------------------------------------+\n| **Filter Base**  | —                     | Low ($120\\text{ Hz} - 300\\text{ Hz}$)|\n+------------------+-----------------------+------------------------------------+\n| **Env Amount**   | —                     | High ($+50\\% \\text{ to } +80\\%$)   |\n+------------------+-----------------------+------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §12 (Recipe C)","<em>Dubstep-Production-Beginner-Live12-Launchkey.md</em>, §6.1–6.2"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"sounddesign-m-4-3-3","code":"4.3","title":"Envelopes as General Modulators","blurb":"- Deploy Pitch Envelopes to synthesize percussive transients for electronic kick drums, 808 sub drops, toms, and laser/zap effects. - Calculate and program ultra-fast pitch drop curves (Pitch = +24 to +48 semitones, Decay = 10 ms - 50 ms).","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"sounddesign-l-o-12","title":"Section overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Learning Objectives","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Deploy <strong>Pitch Envelopes</strong> to synthesize percussive transients for electronic kick drums, 808 sub drops, toms, and laser/zap effects.","Calculate and program ultra-fast pitch drop curves (Pitch = +24 to +48 semitones, Decay = 10 ms - 50 ms).","Route auxiliary envelopes to modulate <strong>Wavetable Position</strong> (<code>Vital</code>, <code>Surge XT</code>) and <strong>LFO Rate/Depth</strong>.","Execute the canonical <strong>One-Motif Four-Role ADSR Challenge</strong>.","Complete the <strong>Gate C Self-Assessment Checkpoint</strong>."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-4-3-1","title":"4.3.1 — Pitch Envelopes: Kicks, 808s, and Transients","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.3.1 — Pitch Envelopes: Kicks, 808s, and Transients","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"While envelopes are most commonly routed to volume (AEG) and filter cutoff (FEG), an <strong>Auxiliary Modulation Envelope</strong> can be mapped directly to <strong>Oscillator Pitch</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">[ PITCH ENVELOPE (Decay = 20 ms, Amount = +36 st) ]\n                       |\n                       v\n            [ OSC 1 PITCH (Sine Wave) ]\n                       |\n                       v\n         [ PURE SINE WAVE KICK TRANSIENT ]</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">Pitch (Hz)\n1 kHz | |\\\n      | | \\\n      | |  \\\n60 Hz | +---+============================= [Sustains at Low Sub Frequency (60 Hz)]\n      0ms 20ms\n      [Transient Click] [Sub Body]</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"When an envelope forces a sine wave to drop from 1000 Hz down to 50 Hz in 20 milliseconds, the human ear does not perceive a musical melody; it perceives a sharp, chest-thumping <strong>acoustic kick transient (click/punch)</strong> followed by a deep sub-bass body."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ultimate Sound Design: Mastering Sound Design</em>, Section 4.8 (Pitch Modulation &amp; Percussion)","<em>Dubstep-Production-Beginner-Live12-Launchkey.md</em>, §6.1"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-4-3-2","title":"4.3.2 — Pitch Envelope Math: Rapid Drop in Semitones","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.3.2 — Pitch Envelope Math: Rapid Drop in Semitones","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Synthesizing professional percussion and 808s requires precision pitch envelope calibration:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+------------------+-----------------------+-------------------+----------------------------------------+\n| Sound Type       | Pitch Env Amount      | Pitch Decay Time  | Oscillator Waveform &amp; Amp Envelope     |\n+------------------+-----------------------+-------------------+----------------------------------------+\n| **Acoustic Kick**| $+24\\text{ to } +36\\text{ st}$| $15\\text{ ms} - 35\\text{ ms}$| Sine wave; Amp Decay $= 250\\text{ ms}$, Sust $= 0$|\n+------------------+-----------------------+-------------------+----------------------------------------+\n| **Trap 808 Bass**| $+12\\text{ to } +24\\text{ st}$| $10\\text{ ms} - 25\\text{ ms}$| Sine wave; Amp Sustain $= 100\\%$ (Long Tail)|\n+------------------+-----------------------+-------------------+----------------------------------------+\n| **Synthetic Tom**| $+12\\text{ st}$       | $80\\text{ ms} - 150\\text{ ms}$| Sine / Triangle; Amp Decay $= 400\\text{ ms}$|\n+------------------+-----------------------+-------------------+----------------------------------------+\n| **Laser / Zap**  | $+48\\text{ st}$       | $40\\text{ ms} - 80\\text{ ms}$| Sawtooth / Square; Resonant Filter     |\n+------------------+-----------------------+-------------------+----------------------------------------+</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Try It (Clean 808 Sub Kick in Drift):","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Load initialized <code>Drift</code>. Set Osc 1 to <strong>Sine</strong>.","In Drift's modulation matrix, route <strong>Env 2</strong> to <code>Pitch</code>.","Set Env 2 parameters: Attack = 0 ms, Decay = 22 ms, Sustain = 0.0, Amount = +24.00.","Set the master Amp Envelope: Attack = 0 ms, Decay = 1.2 s, Sustain = 0.6, Release = 80 ms.","Play F_0 (43.6 Hz). You have synthesized a club-ready, punchy trap 808 from a single sine wave."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>PML Synthesis – 4 – Modulators</em>, Chapter 7 (Pitch Envelope Tuning)","<em>Ableton Live 12 Reference Manual</em>, Chapter 23 (Drift Modulation Routing)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-4-3-3","title":"4.3.3 — Wavetable / Morph Envelope: Position Sweeps","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.3.3 — Wavetable / Morph Envelope: Position Sweeps","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"In wavetable synthesizers (<code>Vital</code>, <code>Surge XT</code>), an envelope can be assigned to modulate <strong>Wavetable Position (Frame Index)</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">[ MOD ENVELOPE (Attack 100ms, Decay 400ms) ] ===&gt; [ VITAL WAVETABLE POSITION ]\n                                                              |\n                                                              v\n                                              [ Scans from Frame 1 to Frame 128 ]</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"As the note plays, the envelope dynamically scrubs through the 3D wavetable, transforming the raw harmonic content from a warm triangle-like shape into a jagged, vocal-like overtone spectrum and back. This provides movement <em>at the source</em> before the signal even reaches the filter."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Vital User Manual</em>, Section 5 (Wavetable Modulation)","<em>Surge XT User Manual</em>, Section 5"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-4-3-4","title":"4.3.4 — Modulating Modulation: Envelope → LFO Rate and Depth","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.3.4 — Modulating Modulation: Envelope → LFO Rate and Depth","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"An advanced sound design technique is using an envelope to modulate the parameters of <em>another</em> modulator (known as <strong>Secondary Modulation</strong>)."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">[ MOD ENVELOPE (Decay = 1.5 s) ] ===&gt; [ LFO 1 DEPTH / RATE ] ===&gt; [ OSCILLATOR PITCH ]\n                                                                        |\n                                                                        v\n                                                         [ Vibrato Increases Over Time ]</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Musical Examples:","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Delayed Vibrato:</strong> Route an envelope with a slow attack (600 ms) to the <strong>Depth</strong> of an LFO controlling pitch. When you hold a lead note, the pitch remains steady at first, and then natural acoustic vibrato blooms into the note after half a second.","<strong>Speeding Wobble:</strong> Route an envelope with a decaying curve to the <strong>Rate</strong> of an LFO controlling filter cutoff. The bass wobble starts extremely fast (1/32nd notes) and decelerates down to a slow grind (1/4th notes) over the course of the bar."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ultimate Sound Design: Mastering Sound Design</em>, Section 4.9 (Modulating Modulators)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-4-3-5","title":"4.3.5 — Noise Envelope: Transient Clicks and Bursts","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.3.5 — Noise Envelope: Transient Clicks and Bursts","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"By routing an auxiliary envelope to the <strong>Volume of a Noise Generator</strong>, you can inject micro-bursts of noise precisely during the first few milliseconds of note onset."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">[ NOISE ENVELOPE (Attack 0ms, Decay 15ms, Sustain 0.0) ] ===&gt; [ NOISE GENERATOR LEVEL ]\n                                                                        |\n                                                                        v\n                                                         [ Injects 15ms Transient Click ]</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"This technique is used to create acoustic hammer-strike realism on synthetic pianos, pick-strike grit on electric basslines, and mallet transients on marimbas and bells."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §2.1 &amp; §12"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-4-3-6","title":"4.3.6 — The One-Motif, Four-Role ADSR Challenge","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.3.6 — The One-Motif, Four-Role ADSR Challenge","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"To prove that envelopes—not presets—define instrument roles, execute the classic sound design laboratory challenge:"}]},{"type":"step","title":"The Challenge:","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Program a 4-note looping MIDI sequence: C_2 - E _2 - G_2 - B _2.","Load an initialized <code>Drift</code> with a single <strong>Sawtooth Wave</strong> and a <strong>Low-Pass Filter (f_c = 400 Hz)</strong> with +5.0 Envelope Depth.","<strong>DO NOT change the oscillator waveform, filter type, or MIDI notes.</strong>","Transform the sound across all four roles using <strong>only ADSR parameter adjustments</strong>:"],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-----------+---------------+---------------+---------------+---------------+\n| Target    | Attack Time   | Decay Time    | Sustain Level | Release Time  |\n+-----------+---------------+---------------+---------------+---------------+\n| **Pluck** | $0\\text{ ms}$ | $200\\text{ ms}$| $0.0$ ($-\\text{inf}$)| $50\\text{ ms}$|\n+-----------+---------------+---------------+---------------+---------------+\n| **Pad**   | $600\\text{ ms}$| $1.2\\text{ s}$| $90\\%$ ($0\\text{ dB}$)| $1.5\\text{ s}$|\n+-----------+---------------+---------------+---------------+---------------+\n| **Organ** | $0\\text{ ms}$ | Irrelevant    | $100\\%$ ($0\\text{ dB}$)| $5\\text{ ms}$|\n+-----------+---------------+---------------+---------------+---------------+\n| **Bass**  | $0\\text{ ms}$ | $300\\text{ ms}$| $40\\%$ ($-6\\text{ dB}$)| $30\\text{ ms}$|\n+-----------+---------------+---------------+---------------+---------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §15 (Exercise B)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-4-3-7","title":"4.3.7 — Gate C: Self-Assessment Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.3.7 — Gate C: Self-Assessment Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Before advancing to <strong>Module 5 (LFO and the Modulation Mindset)</strong>, verify your envelope competencies:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                             GATE C CHECKLIST                                  |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| [ ] 1. The ADSR Principle:                                                    |\n|     Can you explain to another producer why Sustain is an amplitude level     |\n|     while Attack, Decay, and Release are time durations?                      |\n|                                                                               |\n| [ ] 2. The Filter Floor Rule:                                                 |\n|     Can you explain why a filter envelope produces zero timbral movement if   |\n|     the base filter cutoff frequency is left at 20 kHz?                       |\n|                                                                               |\n| [ ] 3. Four-Role Execution:                                                   |\n|     Can you execute the One-Motif Challenge (Lesson 4.3.6), converting a  |\n|     sawtooth source into a Pluck, Pad, Organ, and Bass using only ADSR?       |\n|                                                                               |\n| [ ] 4. Synthetic Kick Design:                                                 |\n|     Can you synthesize a punchy kick drum from scratch using only a pure sine |\n|     wave, a fast pitch envelope (+36 st, 20 ms decay), and an amp envelope?   |\n|                                                                               |\n| [ ] 5. Exponential Curve Utility:                                             |\n|     Can you explain why exponential (concave) decay curves sound punchier and |\n|     more acoustic than linear decay curves?                                   |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"If all 5 criteria are verified by ear and manual execution, <strong>Gate C is passed</strong>. Proceed to <strong>Module 5</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Sound Design Foundations Syllabus</em>, §3 (Gate C Definition)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]}]};
